      "id": "us-federal-holidays",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.govinfo.gov/content/pkg/USCODE-2023-title5/html/USCODE-2023-title5-partIII-subpartE-chap61-subchapI-sec6103.htm",
      "title": "The legal public holidays of 5 U.S.C. § 6103(a), each observed under\n§ 6103(b)(1) and Executive Order 11582 § 3(a) (both set out with the\nsection): a fixed-date holiday on a Saturday is observed the Friday\nbefore, on a Sunday the Monday after. FRCP 6(a)(6)(A), FRAP 26(a)(6)(A)\nand 19 C.F.R. § 201.14(a) all count these days.\n\nEach holiday is in force from the date the vendored section dates it\nto. The fixed-date holidays and Labor Day restate former 5 U.S.C. 87\n(the Act of June 28, 1894), the November 11 holiday former § 87a (Act of\nMay 13, 1938) and Thanksgiving's fourth Thursday former § 87b (Act of\nDecember 26, 1941), per the section's Historical and Revision Notes.\nEarlier history is out of scope: no counting rule here is in force\nbefore 1963.\n\nObserved days. FRCP 6(a)(6)(A) (and before 2009, 6(a)'s \"legal\nholiday\") names \"the day set aside by statute for observing\" each\nholiday. The Sunday-to-Monday shift is validated by a ruling: July 4,\n1993 was a Sunday, and the D.C. Circuit counted \"Monday, July 5, 1993\"\nas a legal holiday (Derrington-Bey v. D.C. Dep't of Corrections, 39\nF.3d 1224, 1225 (D.C. Cir. 1994); sources/change-corpus/rulings, tested\nin frcp6-history-tests.lit). No ruling in that corpus decides a Friday\nbefore a Saturday holiday; it rests on § 6103(b)(1)'s text alone, and\nits direction of risk is a later due date. The vendored section dates\nthe Saturday rule to former § 87c (Pub. L. 86-362, 1959) and the Sunday\nrule to Executive Order 11582 § 3(a) (February 11, 1971), which revoked\nExecutive Order 10358 (1952), whose text is not vendored. Before 1971\nno fixed-date holiday here is observed on another day: a counting rule\nin force 1963-1971 sees only the holiday itself.\n\nNot modeled (FRCP 6(a)(6)(B), (C); FRAP 26(a)(6)(B), (C)): days the\nPresident or Congress declares a holiday, which no vendored source\nlists, and the holidays of the state where the district court sits,\nwhich belong to a district's own calendar and, from 2009, extend only\nforward-counted periods. Where a last day falls on such a day, the date\ncomputed here is the earlier one, never a later one.\n\nThe cite is today's § 6103 (2023 ed.), whose credits and amendment\nnotes date each entry's window: a later text, cited for the whole\nhistory on purpose."
    },
